A previously developed approach by Baker achieved PTAS on various graph classes including planar graphs, but not for chordal graphs. In contrast, techniques by Xiao and Zanotti and research by Burt, Ramanan, and Weisse for chordal graphs relied on less general classes of graph classes.

Here, the team develops a new approach for PTAS on two types of sparse graphs: those with a limited treedepth and bounded degree graphs. The method can also be applied to XP algorithms with a PTAS component, leading to an improved understanding of the complexity of CSPs and CSP-minimization problems with instance-specific parameters that depend on treedepth or degree bounds. The researchers also explore connections to parameterized approximation schemes. The generalised approach results in several new PTAS outcomes, including for Maximum Independent Set, Minimum Vertex Cover, Minimum Linear Arrangement, and Minimum k-Edge Coloring on various graph classes. The method for chordal graphs can be applied to remove the need for non-negative weights in weight-restricted versions of the problems. The techniques may also be used to obtain approximate solutions to minimum treedepth problems. The research on Minimum Vertex Cover also yields an improved XP algorithm with a PTAS component, leading to a bound on the number of cases to consider per parameter value. The outcomes for degree bounds can be found in the full
